Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 2 useful new finds

This run screened 25+ candidates across GitHub, Hugging Face/community
search, Reddit technical threads, pet landmarks/identity, avatar animation,
WebAR, browser rendering, POD/mockups and TTS/viseme tooling. Previous
discoveries were removed. Only two new projects cleared the no-padding bar.
1. LiveToon =E2=80=94 COPY AUTO-RIGGING + PERFORMANCE MAPPING

URL: https://github.com/oaboelazm/LiveToon
Creator: oaboelazm
Activity: 2 commits; repository was freshly indexed/current on 2026-09-20
License: Apache-2.0

Why it matters to Roast.pet:
LiveToon takes a single front-facing cartoon PNG, automatically finds eyes
and mouth using simple dark-pixel/blob analysis, slices the PNG into
animatable regions, then drives those layers from a compact performance
state: mouth open/close, blink, eyebrow, smile, head yaw/pitch/roll. It
also uses a One Euro Filter for jitter smoothing and falls back to
proportional regions if auto-detection fails.

The important part is not its human MediaPipe driver. Roast.pet already has
better plans for audio/visemes and pet geometry. The useful mechanism is
the opposite half: flat PNG -> detect regions -> slice layers -> affine
transforms -> alpha compositor.

That means we should test whether an approved canonical pet PNG can become
a cheap deterministic micro-rig without Live2D, generated video, or
hand-authored layer coordinates.

Install/demo route:
git clone https://github.com/oaboelazm/LiveToon.git && pip install -r
requirements.txt && python app.py; also provides Colab/Kaggle notebook and
CLI.

Dependencies/hardware:
Python, OpenCV/Gradio/MediaPipe-style CV stack; ordinary CPU/webcam
environment. No generation GPU required for the rig/compositor mechanism.

Evidence it works:
The repo exposes separate avatar_rigging, animation_engine,
feature_extraction, and renderer modules; README reports >=3D20 FPS and
documents the exact auto-rigging constraints and fallback behavior.

How much code/work it could replace:
Potentially our first custom sprite-rigging implementation, manual
eye/mouth coordinate setup, affine layer transforms, alpha compositor,
smoothing, and debug tooling.

Integration difficulty: MEDIUM
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM =E2=80=94 only 2 commits, so copy the mechanism ra=
ther
than depend on the project as infrastructure.

Exact experiment:
Take 20 already-clean Roast.pet canonical pet PNGs. Replace LiveToon=E2=80=
=99s
human-avatar region assumptions with our dog/cat landmark coordinates where
available. Auto-extract eyes + muzzle/mouth into layers, then drive five
actions: blink, side-eye, mouth-open, recoil/head-back, tiny idle sway.
Compare identity preservation and setup time against generated I2V and our
existing sprite-state approach. Success criterion: >=3D80% of pets rig
automatically with <30 seconds manual correction and no identity drift.

VERDICT: COPY MECHANISM / FORK EXPERIMENT.
2. lively-mascot =E2=80=94 COPY THE SEMANTIC PERFORMANCE ENGINE

URL: https://github.com/jingluoguo/lively-mascot
Creator: jingluoguo
Activity: 61 commits; current release guidance references v0.3.1
License: MIT

Why it matters to Roast.pet:
This tiny 2-star project is a zero-dependency SVG mascot engine with 40
emotion/reaction states, custom SVG/HTML models, a Web Component, pointer
gaze, 2D/3D presentation, and =E2=80=94 most importantly =E2=80=94 a semant=
ic recipe
system. Emotions are not hardwired to animation IDs. A semantic state such
as celebrating expands into part-level actions such as body=3Dbounce,
eyes=3Dhappy, mouth=3Dsmile, feet=3Dhappy. Models declare which physical ac=
tions
they support.

That is almost exactly the abstraction Roast.pet needs between comedy
direction and rendering:

performance: deadpan_disbelief
-> eyes: side_eye
-> mouth: closed
-> head: recoil_small
-> body: freeze
-> timing: 420ms

The pet renderer can then implement those actions using sprites today,
LiveToon-style affine layers tomorrow, or generated video later. Comedy
choreography remains stable data.

Install/API/demo route:
npm install lively-mascot; framework-free API or <lively-mascot> custom
element; live GitHub Pages demo. Custom SVG/HTML model docs and an
image-to-model skill are included.

Dependencies/hardware:
Zero runtime dependencies; browser CSS/SVG/JS. No GPU required.

Evidence it works:
61 commits, live demo, 40 built-in emotion states, five example characters,
custom model API, runtime action discovery, explicit extension docs and
release pinning guidance.

How much code/work it could replace:
Our bespoke emotion/action registry, renderer-specific choreography calls,
capability negotiation between pet rigs, and potentially much of the
browser-side state machine for deterministic reactions.

Integration difficulty: LOW
Maintenance risk: LOW =E2=80=94 MIT and zero dependencies; pin the implemen=
tation
or copy the semantic schema.

Exact experiment:
Define 10 Roast.pet comedy primitives (deadpan, side_eye, recoil, offended,
smug, confused, double_take, laugh, freeze, look_at_owner) using its recipe
model. Build two render adapters: our current sprite actor and a crude
LiveToon-derived layer rig. Run the same performance JSON through both. If
the script is renderer-agnostic, adopt the schema as the canonical
choreography layer.

VERDICT: COPY MECHANISM / REUSE ENGINE IF FIT IS CLEAN.
Candidates reviewed but rejected this run

WebAR-Youtube (akbartus): neat 7-star MIT MindAR/A-Frame demo projecting
YouTube/CSS3D surfaces onto an image target, but arspace/webar-core already
cover Roast.pet=E2=80=99s AR needs more directly. SKIP.

PET-DINO: strong CVPR 2026 universal visual/text-prompt detector, but too
generic/heavy for the narrow pet pipeline and not a weird cheap replacement
for an immediate Roast.pet task. WATCH ecosystem, not integrate.

CatFLW: useful 48-point cat landmark dataset, but CC-BY-NC means it remains
research-only for commercial Roast.pet and overlaps prior cat-landmark
discoveries. SKIP production.

DogFaceNet: historically useful dog identity work, but older and superseded
for our purposes by the newer nose+face biometric pipeline already found.
SKIP.

Generic mockup generators: several current repos surfaced, but
automated_mockups and the Printify/POD paths found in previous runs already
dominate them. No new win.
TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

   1. A renderer-specific emotion API =E2=80=94 copy lively-mascot=E2=80=99=
s semantic
   emotion -> part-action recipe idea.
   2. Manual per-pet eye/mouth layer coordinates before benchmarking
   LiveToon=E2=80=99s auto-rigging approach with our pet landmarks.
   3. A bespoke affine sprite compositor =E2=80=94 LiveToon already contain=
s the
   mechanical implementation.
   4. A different choreography definition for sprites, Live2D and future
   generated video =E2=80=94 store comedy performances as renderer-independ=
ent data.
   5. Generated video for every blink/side-eye/recoil micro-action =E2=80=
=94
   benchmark deterministic texture-preserving layer animation first.

Most surprising hobby project

lively-mascot. Two stars hides a surprisingly mature 61-commit semantic
animation engine. The useful invention is not its cartoon cat; it is
separating what the character is doing emotionally from how this particular
rig physically renders it.
Fastest integration win

Define the ten canonical Roast.pet reactions in lively-mascot-style recipe
JSON and make the existing sprite actor consume them. This requires no
model downloads, inference, customer data or paid API calls and immediately
tests whether choreography can become durable data.
Repo/author worth following

jingluoguo / lively-mascot. The repo is actively iterating on exactly the
abstraction Roast.pet needs between AI/comedy direction and a replaceable
visual renderer.
Architecture implication

The emerging Roast.pet stack should separate three durable objects:

   1. pet.json =E2=80=94 identity, canonical texture, landmarks/geometry.
   2. performance.json =E2=80=94 semantic comedy state + timed part-level a=
ctions.
   3. renderer adapter =E2=80=94 sprites / affine micro-rig / Live2D / I2V.

Then the roast script can say Fred performs deadpan_disbelief at 2.4s
without knowing whether Fred is currently rendered as sprites or tomorrow=
=E2=80=99s
video model. That is much more durable than accumulating renderer-specific
animation code.

